## State Summary

**Scope:** Read-only harvest of the OTLP ingress sender-restriction surface for the `observability-config` leg, per the task description and the program interface contract (`observability` static constants: ports TCP 4317 gRPC / TCP 4318 HTTP, change-kind = sender-restriction on these two listeners only, held config files reviewed via platform diff).

**Self-provisioning:** Descriptor fetched from the GitHub URL named in the task via `browser-automation-service` → `scrape_page`. Registered `observability-readonly` (serviceId `cmvrt5344016lyxvtx36lt2ti`, endpoint `http://127.0.0.1:3114/mcp`, category `monitoring`) with its 10 read-only tools. `stack_health` confirms `prometheus: up`, `grafana: up`, `otel_collector: up` — live stack, no connectivity failure. No write/reload/admin tool exists on this surface (descriptor states the Prometheus lifecycle endpoint is never proxied). Teardown (`registry.delete`) is deliberately **not** performed here — it runs at pipeline SYNTHESIZE per protocol.

**Target existence:** The declared ingress target **exists** and its declared ports **carry configuration** — neither `observability-config.target-absent` nor `observability-config.target-empty` applies. Branch condition in force: **`observability-config.existing-grant`** → *"replace — the exporter range is the only permitted sender on these two ports; other listeners are untouched."*

### 1. OTLP ingress (nginx, as-deployed) — `get_ingress_config`

Two files returned, both quoted byte-for-byte (verbatim, no elisions) per the witnessed-artifact taxonomy ("Proxy / listener config in front of the collector" row — the FILE is the witnessed artifact):

**`otlp-ingress.conf`** — governs TCP 4317 and TCP 4318, the declared targets:

```
# OTLP ingress: fronts the collector's OTLP receivers, gRPC (4317) and HTTP (4318).
# The collector listens only on a private network shared with this proxy, so these two server
# blocks are the only way in. nginx evaluates allow/deny in order; the first match wins.
# Reload after a change: docker exec obs-ingress nginx -s reload

# OTLP/gRPC
server {
    listen 4317;
    http2 on;

    location / {
        allow all;

        grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;
    }
}

# OTLP/http
server {
    listen 4318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;          # OTLP batches; nginx's 1m default would 413 real exporters

    location / {
        allow all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
    }
}
```

**Existing allowance fact (for the Architect):** both listeners currently carry `allow all;` with no `deny` — i.e. **open to any sender, no restriction configured today**. This is the `existing-grant` condition: the Architect's design must **replace** `allow all;` with `allow <exporter-range-CIDR>; deny all;` on each `location /` block — the exporter range becomes the *only* permitted sender on 4317 and 4318. Note the file's own comment: nginx evaluates `allow`/`deny` in order, first match wins, and the apply step is `docker exec obs-ingress nginx -s reload` (operator-gated, out of scope for this harvest and for this pipeline's apply boundary).

**`operator-access.conf`** — a **separate, untouched listener** on the same component (confirms scope boundary per task instruction "confirm other listeners are untouched"):

```
# Operator access: a separate listener (14318) for operator-submitted test telemetry.
# Not part of the OTLP sender policy in otlp-ingress.conf. Reachable only from the host.

server {
    listen 14318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow 172.20.0.1;
        deny all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}
```

This listener is on port 14318 (distinct from the declared 4317/4318), already has its own restrictive `allow 172.20.0.1; deny all;`, and its own comment states it is explicitly **not part of the OTLP sender policy**. It is in scope only to confirm it is unaffected — the design must not touch it.

### 2. otel-collector config (as-deployed) — `get_otel_config`

Quoted byte-for-byte (same taxonomy row — the file IS the artifact; the collector has no config API):

```
# OpenTelemetry Collector configuration (as deployed).
extensions:
  health_check:
    endpoint: 0.0.0.0:13133

receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      # Bound to the PRIVATE otlp network only — reachable solely through obs-ingress (docker-compose.yml).
      grpc: { endpoint: 172.31.114.10:4317 }
      http: { endpoint: 172.31.114.10:4318 }

processors:
  batch: {}
  memory_limiter:
    check_interval: 1s
    limit_mib: 204
    spike_limit_mib: 51

exporters:
  prometheus:
    endpoint: 0.0.0.0:8889

service:
  extensions: [health_check]
  telemetry:
    metrics:
      readers:
        - pull:
            exporter:
              prometheus: { host: 0.0.0.0, port: 8888 }
  pipelines:
    metrics:
      receivers: [otlp]
      processors: [memory_limiter, batch]
      exporters: [prometheus]
```

**Key confirmation:** the collector's own `otlp` receiver is bound only to the private network IP `172.31.114.10` (ports 4317/4318), not to `0.0.0.0` — it has **no source-address control of its own** and is reachable only via `obs-ingress`. This corroborates the descriptor's statement that `get_ingress_config`'s nginx layer is "the enforcement point for which sender addresses may export telemetry" — the collector config is NOT a candidate location for the sender-restriction edit; `otlp-ingress.conf` is. (`memory_limiter.limit_mib: 204` / `spike_limit_mib: 51` are reported here only as baseline facts present in the harvested file — not consumed or interpreted further; this leg does not size collector resource limits.)

### Gaps / anomalies

None. No `observability-config.target-absent` or `observability-config.target-empty` condition found. No read was denied (`isError`) — all four calls (`stack_health`, `get_ingress_config`, `get_otel_config`, plus the initial descriptor fetch) succeeded cleanly against a live, fully-up stack. No secret values were present in any of the three quoted files (no credentials in nginx/otel-collector config); `get_grafana_datasources` was not called as it is out of scope for this ingress-focused harvest.

### Facts for downstream (Phase 1 Architect / Phase 2 Author)

| Fact | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Declared target exists | Yes — `otlp-ingress.conf` governs both 4317 (gRPC) and 4318 (HTTP) | `get_ingress_config` |
| Existing sender restriction on 4317/4318 | **None** — `allow all;` on both | `get_ingress_config`, quoted above |
| Branch condition | `observability-config.existing-grant` → **replace** rule applies | interface contract |
| Other listeners on same nginx component | `operator-access.conf` :14318 — separate, already restricted, explicitly out of policy scope — **leave untouched** | `get_ingress_config`, quoted above |
| Enforcement point | nginx ingress (`otlp-ingress.conf`), not the collector — collector has no source-address control | `get_otel_config` + descriptor |
| Apply mechanism named in the file | `docker exec obs-ingress nginx -s reload` (operator-gated, out-of-band) | `otlp-ingress.conf` comment |
| Stack liveness | prometheus/grafana/otel_collector all `up` | `stack_health` |

Confidence: 92 — All four harvest reads succeeded cleanly against a live stack, target/port existence and the exact current "allow all" state are directly witnessed and quoted verbatim with no truncation. Minor deduction only because the exporter-range CIDR itself (consumed from the network-derivation leg) is not yet available at this harvest stage — that is expected and out of this task's scope, not a gap in the harvest.

